  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simplified domain map of the RIP1 protein, shown from the N terminus on the left to the C terminus on the right. It highlights three main structural regions arranged along the protein: an N‑terminal kinase domain, a long intermediate domain, and a C‑terminal death domain. Amino acid positions are marked to show where these regions begin and end, indicating that the kinase domain occupies the first part of the protein, the intermediate region spans the middle, and the death domain lies near the end.\n\nWithin the intermediate domain, a short segment labeled RHIM is emphasized, suggesting it is an important motif embedded in this central region. The figure also marks a site around residue 324 where caspase 8 cleaves RIP1, illustrated with scissors. This visually conveys a key regulatory mechanism: RIP1 is not only organized into functional domains, but can also be processed by caspase 8 at a specific position.\n\nOverall, the image summarizes how RIP1 combines signaling modules involved in kinase activity, protein interactions, and death signaling, with proteolytic cleavage appearing as an important control point.",
